\subsubsection{Frequency model with deductible}
Suppose $N^*$ and $N$ marginally follow the negative binomial distribution. Note that it is known that if $N^*|D$ belongs to $(a,b,m)$ family for arbitrary $m \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{ 0 \}$, then we may have a simplified expression of $g(n|d;\bm{x})$ from \citet{Klugman2012}. For example, negative binomial distribution with parameters $r$ and $\lambda$, which is denoted by $\mathcal{NB}(r, \lambda)$ has the following probability function:
$$
f(n)=	 {n+r-1 \choose n} \cdot \left( \frac{1}{1+\lambda} \right)^{r} \left( \frac{\lambda}{1+\lambda} \right)^{n},
$$
\rev{with mean $r\lambda$ and variance $r\lambda(1+\lambda)$,} and it naturally belongs to $(a,b,0)$ family. Therefore, if $N^*|D, \bm{x} \sim \mathcal{NB}(r, \lambda(\bm{x}))$, then
\begin{equation} 
\begin{aligned}  \label{eq:fg}
g(n|d;\bm{x})=	 {n+r-1 \choose n} \cdot \left( \frac{1}{1+p(d;\bm{x})\lambda(\bm{x})} \right)^{r} \left( \frac{p(d;\bm{x})\lambda(\bm{x})}{1+p(d;\bm{x})\lambda(\bm{x})} \right)^{n},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
so that $N|D=d; \bm{x} \sim \mathcal{NB}(r, p(d;\bm{x}) \lambda(\bm{x}))$.

\rev{The frequency models considered in this article are summarized in the lower panel of Table~\ref{tab:modelsummary}. In all of them, the number of loss events marginally follows $N^*|\bm{x} \sim \mathcal{NB}(r, \exp(\bm{x}\beta_{N}) /r)$, so that $\mathbb{E}[N^*|\bm{x}]=\exp(\bm{x}\beta_{N})$. Under full or censored reporting the response is $N=N^*$ and the likelihood component is \eqref{eq:fr}; under truncated reporting the response is the number of payments $N=\sum_{j=1}^{N^*} I(Y^*_{j}>d)$ and the likelihood component is \eqref{eq:urt}, which reduces to \eqref{eq:fg} so that $\mathbb{E}[N|D=d; \bm{x}]=p(d;\bm{x}) \cdot \mathbb{E}[N^*|D=d;\bm{x}]$. Under the exogeneity assumption, $C_{DN}$ is the independence copula and $f_{N^*|D}=f_{N^*}$ (marginal frequency models FEM1 and FEM2 for the full/censored and truncated reporting scenarios, respectively); under endogeneity, $f_{N^*|D}$ is given by \eqref{eq:FCM} for a continuous deductible (joint frequency models FCM1 and FCM2) or by \eqref{eq:FDM} for a discrete deductible (joint frequency models FDM1 and FDM2). The truncated frequency models FEM2, FCM2 and FDM2 require the retention probability $p(d;\bm{x})$, which is computed from the estimated severity model of the corresponding reporting scenario and deductible treatment (SEM3, SCM3 and SDM3, respectively), in line with the sequential estimation of Section 2.3. Since the truncated marginal frequency model inherits any bias in the severity model through $p(d;\bm{x})$, we additionally consider the variant FEM2G, in which $p(d;\bm{x})$ is computed from the ground-up severity model SEM1, in order to isolate the effect of the severity model on the truncated frequency model.}

As in the severity models, one can assume that the deductible $D$ is exogenous to the claim frequency so that \rev{$N^* \perp D|\bm{x}$}, then FEM1 is equivalent to 
FEM2, %$N|\bm{x} \,{\buildrel d \over =}\,  N|D, \bm{x}$ since $N|D, \bm{x} \sim \mathcal{NB}(r,  p(\bm{x}) \cdot \exp(\bm{x}\beta_{N}) /r)$
according to the argument in (\ref{eq:fg}). Therefore, if the exogenous assumption holds, then the estimates of $\beta_{N}$ from FEM1 and FEM2 should be also quite close. However, once we consider endogeneity of $D$ (that implies \rev{$N^*|\bm{x} \not\perp D|\bm{x}$}), \rev{the joint frequency models FCM1/FCM2 (continuous deductible) or FDM1/FDM2 (discrete deductible) are needed to capture the endogeneity of the deductible.}

\subsubsection{Ratemaking with frequency and severity models and reporting scenarios}

\rev{The pure premium given deductible $D=d$ is the expected sum of the insurer's actual payouts, which can be written in two equivalent ways:}
\begin{align}
	{\rm Premium} = {\rm E}(S|D=d;\bm{x})&={\rm E}(N^*|D=d;\bm{x}) \times {\rm E}(\{Y^*-d\}_+\ |\ D=d;\bm{x})\label{eq:censoredpremium}\\
    &={\rm E}(N^*|D=d;\bm{x}) \times \rev{p(d;\bm{x})}\times{\rm E}(\rev{Y^*-d\ |\ Y^*>d,}\ D=d;\bm{x})\nonumber\\
    &={\rm E}(N|D=d;\bm{x}) \times {\rm E}(Y^*-d\ |\ Y^*>d,\ D=d;\bm{x})\label{eq:truncatedpremium}
\end{align}
where $N = \sum_{\rev{j}=1}^{N^*}I(Y^{*}_{\rev{j}}>d)$\rev{, the second equality uses ${\rm E}[(Y^*-d)_+|D=d;\bm{x}]=\Pr(Y^*>d|D=d;\bm{x})\,{\rm E}[Y^*-d|Y^*>d,D=d;\bm{x}]$ with $p(d;\bm{x})=\Pr(Y^*>d|D=d;\bm{x})$, and the third equality uses ${\rm E}(N|D=d;\bm{x})=p(d;\bm{x})\,{\rm E}(N^*|D=d;\bm{x})$, which follows from the binomial thinning in \eqref{eq:urt}}. While \rev{the} full reporting scenario provides both pure premiums by (\ref{eq:censoredpremium}) and (\ref{eq:truncatedpremium}), the pure premium can be estimated by (\ref{eq:censoredpremium}) under \rev{the} censored reporting scenario and \rev{by} (\ref{eq:truncatedpremium}) under \rev{the} truncated reporting scenario.
